    "evidence_text": "As suggested by the results of an ablation experiment, averaging is better than summing in our application (see subsection SECREF30).\n\nThe normalized alignment coefficients are finally used to compute the attentional vector $\\mathbf {u}^T \\in \\mathbb {R}^d$ as a weighted sum of the final representations $\\mathbf {\\hat{H}}^T$.\n\nNote that we tried with multiple context vectors, i.e., with a matrix $\\mathbf {V}^T$ instead of a vector $\\mathbf {v}^T$, like in , but results were not convincing, even when adding a regularization term\n\nsee in Table TABREF29 that having more iterations improves performance.\n\nto better performance, while on Polarity and IMDB, it is the opposite.\n\nRemoving the master node deteriorates performance across all datasets, clearly showing the value of having such a node.\n\nAs shown in Table TABREF29, using sum instead of mean decreases performance everywhere, suggesting that in our application, capturing the distribution of neighbor representations is more important that capturing their structure.\n\nExcept on Reuters (almost no change), performance always suffers, stressing the need to take into account the root node during updates, not only its neighborhood.",
